WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.830
Good morning John, it's Friday. It's Question… Tuesday? The day that we answer real questions from real Nerdfighters?

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:08.790
Why do you always get to do question videos just 'cause you do videos on Tuesdays? Not fair.

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:13.280
I went to Twitter. I got questions. Here they are. "Are numbers real, or did we make them up?"

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:14.940
Uhhhhh.

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:17.560
[whispering] I feel like we started out hard!

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:20.100
Question videos are supposed to be easier!

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:21.180
[breathless] I…

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:25.060
Okay? Next question? "What's the best book you've read lately?" Yeah,

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:27.920
I often don't like to promote books by…

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:33.660
dead people, because there are so many great books being written by people who are working right now.

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:38.840
But, the actual answer to this question is "Dawn" by Octavia Butler, part of the Xenogenesis trilogy.

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:43.180
And it is somewhat timely, because it's being turned into a TV series by Ava Duvernay.

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:48.420
And this is, shockingly, the first time an Octavia Butler work is being adapted for TV

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:53.580
or movies… ever?! How did that happen? Anyway, "Dawn" is very good and it gives enormous

00:00:53.580 --> 00:01:00.680
insight into what it… m—is like to be a human, and also what "human" means, which is…

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:06.520
Yeah. "How is book editing going?" You know, not great. Uh, it's been a busy week.

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:12.420
I have a deadline that I may miss, but I'm workin' on it. It's a lot of stress in my life right now.

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:17.180
"What is our official emoji slash emoji combination for your new book, Hank?"

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:21.180
Well, I!…don't know, so let's ask people to come up with stuff.

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:24.749
So we'll come back to that at the end of the video. My book is called "An Absolutely Remarkable Thing"

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:27.629
It's available for preorder now, and it will come out on September 25th.

00:01:27.629 --> 00:01:32.680
"How do I stay informed of current events without having them take over my life and stress me out?" If you figure that one out,

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:35.980
I…I think that all of us would really like to know your answer.

00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:40.780
"What was the first thing you did after you finished writing your first book?" I sat and watched a French guy play his

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:43.800
accordion in the airport while his little French children

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:47.220
ran around and were silly, and thought about how a lot of weird things happen in my book,

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:50.440
but also a lot of weird things happen in the real world. And then I got on a plane and I went home.

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:52.640
I think that was Po—I think I was going home from PodCon.

00:01:52.649 --> 00:01:55.379
Which is interesting, 'cause the next question: "What is this adorable rainbow

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:57.930
I saw under the plane wing on the flight home from PodCon?"

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:02.360
That's called a glory, and it's very similar to a rainbow, in that it's an effect of the

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:09.080
diffraction of light when it hits water droplets. But a glory happens with smaller droplets of water than a rainbow, and you have to be

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:12.200
between the Sun and the cloud

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:17.100
in order for you to see a glory, which means you don't usually see one when you're on the ground.

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:20.800
It can happen, though, and so glories were seen before planes happened,

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:26.860
Mostly from people who were on the tops of mountains, which is really cool. And also, those people were often inside of the cloud,

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:31.340
so there was fog, and so in addition to the glory you get what's called a Brocken spectre,

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:38.540
which is very cool, and a super creepy thing that you're seeing right now, right? Creepy! "Can you sing your last text message?"

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:41.060
(singing) Good morning to things!

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:43.500
(singing) Today is Julie's birthday.

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:49.000
(Singing) And also will you be able to record Dadcast on Monday?

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:52.460
Yes, I can record Dadcast on Monday, and happy birthday, Julie.

00:02:52.460 --> 00:02:58.220
Thanks, Marianne, for keeping my life in order. "If you could wear any Elton John outfit, which one would it be?"

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:03.860
I—don't know! We—I guess we have to go look at Elton John outfits now. How big is he? Five-eight.

00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:07.820
Um, feedback. No errors, just give general feedback,

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:09.920
Um—This is helpful.

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:15.900
Thanks, Google. I mean it said if I *could,* so if I could, I don't know. 'Elton John outfits.'

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:20.400
Um… that one's real good. With like, Beetlejuice, but with a sparkly banana.

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:23.030
Think it would be real hard to see with this one. My God, now

00:03:23.030 --> 00:03:27.860
I want to do an Elton John outfit review podcast. I definitely cannot compete with the chest hair.

00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:33.780
"I play piano and also *am* a piano!" Yeah, I think I'm gonna go with Sparkly Banana Beetlejuice, and, actually, that reminds me,

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:39.200
It's time to look up our emoji sequence! Uh, Katherine says that she has one but it has spoilers,

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:45.820
so that's the advantage of being Katherine, and also April May says "robot dark-haired white girl Statue of Liberty burrito,"

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:49.820
and, you know, feel like she knows what she's talkin' about. John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

